@question {{id}} | {{title}}
@template choices.v1
@version 1
@goal {{objective}}
@given {{given}}
@domain B is obtained only by swapping the two rows of A.
@read {{reading_id}}
@step 10 | How does a row swap change the determinant?
{{choices_1}}
@feedback {{same_id}} | A row swap negates rather than retains the value: -({{determinant}})={{swapped_determinant}}, not {{determinant}}.
@feedback {{zero_id}} | A row swap preserves whether a determinant is zero. Here the swap gives -({{determinant}})={{swapped_determinant}}, so this zero conclusion is unsupported.
@after {{after_1}}
@wrong Recalculate both determinants from their displayed row orders.
@why A row swap changes only the determinant's sign: \det(A)={{determinant_expression}}={{determinant}},\quad \det(B)={{swapped_expression}}={{swapped_determinant}}. Thus \det(B)=-\det(A)=-({{determinant}})={{swapped_determinant}}.
@end
